# =============================================================================
# CITS5206 Capstone — line endings and file handling
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Windows writes CRLF line endings, macOS and Linux write LF. Git stores bytes,
# so the same line saved on two platforms is two different lines to Git. Left
# alone, that produces pull requests where every line of a file is marked as
# changed and the one real edit is invisible — and it resets `git blame` for
# every affected line, which is how we evidence who wrote what.
#
# core.autocrlf cannot fix this: it lives on each person's machine and is not
# shared. This file is committed, so it applies to everyone automatically, on
# every clone, without anyone having to configure anything.
#
# POLICY: everything is LF, in the repository and in the working tree.
# Our content is Markdown, HTML and (soon) web code. VS Code, PowerShell,
# browsers and every toolchain we are likely to pick handle LF correctly.
#
# Check what applies to a path:   git check-attr -a <path>
# After changing this file:       git add --renormalize .
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default — detect text automatically, store and check out as LF
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explicit text types
#
# Redundant with `text=auto` in the normal case, but it removes the guesswork
# for files with unusual content — a Markdown file that happens to start with a
# long base64 block, for instance, can be misdetected as binary.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.jsx       text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.tsx       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.jsonc     text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.csv       text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files that MUST keep a specific ending regardless of the policy above
#
# A shell script with CRLF fails on Linux — the shebang line becomes
# `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the interpreter is reported as not found. This matters the
# moment we add CI.
#
# Windows batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe needs CRLF.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never convert, never try to diff
#
# Most of these are excluded by .gitignore, but the declaration still matters:
# anything force-added with `git add -f` would otherwise be run through line
# ending conversion, which silently corrupts it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.eot       binary
*.zip       binary
*.docx      binary
*.xlsx      binary
*.pptx      binary
*.m4a       binary
*.mp3       binary
*.wav       binary
*.mp4       binary
*.mov       binary


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub language statistics
#
# Without this, GitHub reports the repository as almost entirely HTML, because
# the presentation decks are large self-contained HTML files. That would be a
# misleading first impression of a project whose application code lives in
# src/. Marking decks and reference material as documentation keeps the
# language bar meaningful once src/ has content in it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
presentations/*.html    linguist-documentation
reference/**            linguist-documentation
docs/**                 linguist-documentation
